    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d407">

                <pb facs="0479" n="473" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg473"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 European War 1939/643: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dickerson</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-11-05T14:00:00+03:00">November 5, 1942—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>982. <hi rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Izvestiya</hi> of November 4 publish a ukase [of] the Presidium [of] the
                    Supreme Soviet of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics forming an
                    extraordinary state commission for ascertaining and investigating, with a view
                    to obtaining ultimate and suitable compensation therefor, the offenses of the
                    German Fascist aggressors and their accomplices, and all the losses in civilian
                    life and property inflicted by them upon citizens’ collective farms, public
                    organizations, state enterprises and institutions of the Soviet Union. The
                    losses referred to will also include expense and dislocation occasioned by the
                    removal of industrial enterprises, farm and other property, and sections of the
                    population.</p>
                <p>This commission which will coordinate and expand work already being done in this
                    field, includes in its membership: Shvernik,<anchor n="51" xml:id="d407-f1"/>
                    head of the Soviet labor unions, as chairman; Zhdanov,<anchor n="52"
                        xml:id="d407-f2"/> Secretary of the Central Committee of the Communist
                    Party; Nikolai, Metropolitan of Kiev and Galicia; A. Tolstoi<anchor n="53"
                        xml:id="d407-f3"/> the writer; several prominent academicians; and a famous
                    aviatrix.</p>
                <p>The two papers mentioned and also <hi rendition="#italic">Krasnaya Zvszda</hi> at
                    the same time publish editorials concerning the new commission but these appear
                    to contain nothing of special interest. They refer, however, to the note
                    transmitted in translation as the Embassy’s 905 October 19, 10 a.m.,<anchor
                        n="54" xml:id="d407-f4"/> as well as to the three circular atrocity notes
                    addressed by Molotov to the chiefs of diplomatic missions during the past year,
                    as reported telegraphically in each case at the time.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dickerson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d407-f1" n="51" xml:id="d407-f1-content">
                <p>Nikolay Mikhailovich Shvernik, president of the All-Union Central Council of
                    Labor Unions.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d407-f2" n="52" xml:id="d407-f2-content">
                <p>Andrey Alexandrovich Zhdanov.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d407-f3" n="53" xml:id="d407-f3-content">
                <p>Alexey Tolstoy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d407-f4" n="54" xml:id="d407-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed; but see telegram No. 896, October 16, from the Chargé in the Soviet
                    Union, <ref target="frus1942v01#pg_60">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>,
                        p. 60</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
